<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nala dynasty</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nala dynasty ruled parts of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, around the Bastar region of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, from about the fifth century. Known from coins and inscriptions, the Nalas fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakatakas</a> and were among the powers defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtivarman-i">Kirtivarman I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> in the late sixth century as the Chalukyas expanded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Nalas are known from the inscriptions of three kings, Arthapati, Bhavadatta and Skandavarman, and from the gold coins of three more, Varaharaja, Nandanaraja and Stambha. These coins do not name the dynasty, but they carry the Nala bull-and-crescent emblem, match the known Nala coins in weight, give their issuers' names in the box-headed script and were all found in former Nala territory. Palaeography suggests that Varaharaja was the earliest of the kings, though not necessarily the founder.</p><p>Bhavadatta's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate, found at Rithpur in Amravati district, was issued from Nandivardhana, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka</a> capital, which suggests that he extended Nala rule into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a> at the expense of the Vakatakas. In his last years he suffered reverses: an inscription of his son Skandavarman indicates that he lost Pushkari, the Vakataka king Prithivisena II is said to have restored his family's glory, apparently by defeating the Nalas, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aihole">Aihole</a> inscription credits the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirtivarman-i">Kirtivarman I</a> with destroying them. Skandavarman's Podagada inscription records that he recovered the family's glory, repopulated the deserted city of Pushkari and built a shrine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Their capital was probably Pushkari, named as the place of issue of Arthapati's inscription. It was long thought to be Podagada in Koraput district and has since been identified with Garhdhanora in Bastar district, where excavations by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>'s Directorate of Archaeology and Museums found ruined temples and sculptures. The inscriptions invoke Maheshvara (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>) and Mahasena (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Kartikeya</a>), and the kings patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaktism">Shaktism</a>. One theory links the family name to Nala, the king of the Nishadha Kingdom in legend, but there is no firm proof.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Nothing is known of Skandavarman's immediate successors. The Nalas were probably supplanted by the Panduvamshi dynasty, and the Sharabhapuriyas, their northern neighbours, whose coins resemble theirs, may also have contributed to their decline. A dynasty claiming descent from Nala of legend later ruled a small area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>; its undated Rajim inscription belongs to the 7th or 8th century, and its known members were Prithviraja, Viruparaja and Vilasatunga. Descendants of the Nalas are also said to have founded the Khidisingi mandala in the Ganjam region around the 9th or 10th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nala dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Kirtivarman I (r. c. 566–597) defeated the Kadambas, the <b>Nalas</b> and the Mauryas of the Konkan, turning a local principality into the leading power of the western Deccan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
